      "math_academy_id": "e-9829",
      "problem": "For the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14132012/assets/5e8d464fd7e3e4c3ed2678158869eeabf3da78fa895732546f29e049b9f9e597.svg) find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$",
      "worked_solution": "For an \"almost\" zero $m \\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n \\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{6}^{-1} & \\,\\,\\,0 & 0 \\\\ \\,\\,0 & \\,{4}^{-1} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
